 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to children's non-regulated\ncamps",
    "basePrintNo": "S6063",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6063-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1394-d, 1392-a, 1394-a & 1394-b, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6063\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to children's non-re-\ngulated camps\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require single-purpose day camps and camps regulated by DOH to check\nthe DOJ national sex offender database for all potential employees and\nvolunteers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the public health law by adding a new section 1394-c,\nrequiring the operators of children's non-regulated camps to ascertain\nwhether an employee or volunteer is listed on the national sex offender\nregistry using the DOJ public database prior to the date an employee or\nvolunteer begins work at such camp.\n\nSections three, four, and five amend sections 1392a, 1394a, and 1394b to\nrequire camps regulated by DOH to check whether an employee or volunteer\nis listed on the national sex offender registry using the DOJ public\ndatabase prior to the date the employee or volunteer begins work at a\ncamp.\n\nSection six states the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChapter 248 of the Laws of 2022 established additional background check\nrequirements for camps. The law created a requirement for both DOH regu-\nlated and single-purpose camps to run all employees and volunteers\nthrough the national sex offender registry using the National Crime\nInformation Center (NCIC) database.\n\nHowever, in New York, only law enforcement and DCJS may access this\ninformation through NCIC. This legislation amends Chapter 248 of 2022 to\ninstead require camps to conduct a national sex offender registry search\nthrough the DOJ public database. DOH regulated camps already use this\ndatabase. This chapter amendment is necessary to ensure that the\noriginal intent of the law is preserved and that camps use a system that\nis accessible to them to conduct background checks.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law.\n",
